{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1357386-bonuses-for-dance-of-the-toys","id":1357386,"title":"Bonuses for Dance Of The Toys","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-12-28T12:50:27-08:00","id":1357386,"title":"Bonuses for Dance Of The Toys","content":"As the title implies, I\u2019ve been making Seeming bonuses for Dance Of The Toys, as we only have the two per normal. However, I\u2019m a little iffy on a couple of them, so I figured I\u2019d post them here to get some feedback.<br /> <br /> <i>Darkling</i>: Shadows are a Wisp\u2019s best friend, and act where her hands will not. She may evoke the contract on any object she can touch the shadow of, or spend a point of Willpower to do so with her own shadow touching, the device.<br /> <b>Notes</b>: This is a pretty situational bonus, admittedly, and I\u2019m not sure how well it would work for a Fae Touched given that their effective range for controlling an object with Dance Of The Toys is 0 (meaning they\u2019d have to be constantly touching it). However, I have always liked the thought of a Darkling being able to use the contract from a range. Not entirely sure if this should have an additional Glamour cost to it instead of a Willpower cost.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Elemental</i>: The Sprite invites the object into his domain, granting it the same comfort he has in his own element. While surrounded by an instance of his associated element, the object slowly rejuvenates from damaged sustained. Damage caused by Bashing or Lethal sources is repaired at one point per scene, but damage from Aggravated sources require a full day of exposure per point without being used and is only practical for the permanently enchanted.<br /> <b>Notes</b>: This is by far the trickiest bonus I have had to make, and even now I\u2019m not entirely sure this shouldn\u2019t be swapped with the Ogre bonus instead. It never felt right to me to make Dance Of The Toys work on an element instead of an object, or grant an object additional movement it couldn\u2019t make (working on statues and such) since that takes it out of its niche and makes it far too abusable by bad actors. I want to try making an option that doesn\u2019t use elements directly but alludes to them, but I can\u2019t figure a good benefit beyond toughness or regeneration. Open to suggestions for this one.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Fairest</i>: The Muse infuses a part of her own personality into the mechanisms, inspiring them to bring out all their effort and become more competent. After a meaningful interaction with a permanently enchanted object, the player may purchase an additional dot of Retainer for it. This, along with the initial dot(s), is subject to Sanctity of Merits should the object be destroyed or the enchantment undone.<br /> <b>Notes</b>: This was the first bonus I had settled on, and was inspired by someone pointing out on the forums that normally it\u2019s not entirely reasonable to be allowed to increase an enchanted object\u2019s Retainer value since, well, it\u2019s an object. However, I wanted to avoid making it an easy means of getting an object that could do a task with 10 dice, hence requiring a meaningful interaction with said object. Loving the Love Bug movies that I do, I am tempted to throw in a note about how Fairest often find their Toys to act on their own without a command when they get to a high Retainer level, but I feel that\u2019s a bit overboard.<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>Ogre</i>: A Gargoyle is meant to stand the test of time, and always weather what comes of them. Objects affected by this contract gain 1 Durability, and inflict a -2 to those who would attempt to stop them doing their tasks.<br /> <b>Notes</b>: This is a bonus that I\u2019m reasonably satisfied with, but I\u2019m still not sure if I\u2019ve gotten a good balance for it. Someone suggested I make this bonus instead grant an object additional Structure equal to the user\u2019s Wyrd, but that would be a waste for a Fae Touched so I\u2019m not too fond of it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <u>Edit 1:</u><br /> <br /> <b>*</b> Reworded Fairest blessing to be more explicitly about buying additional dots of Retainer and not upgrading a supernatural power emulating one.<br /> <b>*</b> Added a Willpower cost to part of the Darkling blessing simply to avoid players from doing free tomfoolery such as standing on a bridge, extending their hand and commanding cars to crash into one another. I have played with those who have done far worse for LOLs with a free power; I will not enable it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5803,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","user":"Taidragon","date":". 01-01-2020, 04:50 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-28T20:05:43-08:00","id":1357444,"title":"","content":"I think Fae Touched should have gotten at least Wyrd 1 or a merit that allowed them to count as wyrd one for the purpose of contracts with effects that are based on Wyrd. Or maybe just make the rule these contracts have a miniumal effect of 1.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-12-29T02:48:42-08:00","id":1357493,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1357386-bonuses-for-dance-of-the-toys?p=1357444#post1357444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think Fae Touched should have gotten at least Wyrd 1 or a merit that allowed them to count as wyrd one for the purpose of contracts with effects that are based on Wyrd. Or maybe just make the rule these contracts have a miniumal effect of 1.</div> </div> </div> </div>I honestly think it\u2019s appropriate. These are people who are, through some loophole or another, being given access to abilities and powers that are bound to beings who have earned the enmity of Iron. Out of all the Fae we know of with Contracts, Fae Touched are both without the bane of Iron and also with the barest of ties via the Wyrd. Their magic is subtle, to the point they can\u2019t even evoke the Loophole of Contracts, but they are still able to use the Wyrd as a conduit and have the right to Contracts and to learn them via a tutor. It\u2019s why I like that, from a design perspective, most effects aren\u2019t scaled by Wyrd, and even those that are are given some means of using the contract.<br /> <br /> Tldr: I don\u2019t see it as a big fuss, but I am still keeping it in mind when I design these so that any Fae Touched characters that may use them won\u2019t be effectively locked out of an option because their Wyrd is non-existent.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-02T05:42:35-08:00","id":1358401,"title":"","content":"I'm past the halfway point of coming up with 4 Seeming bonuses for every Contract, below are the ones I have for Dance of the Toys. I like my Darkling &amp; Elemental ones, so you could check them out if you're looking to replace yours (and a note for the Elemental one you have: objects don't normally track aggravated damage separate from any other damage, so doing that may lead to extra bookkeeping, which I try to avoid in these benefits for elegance).<br /> <br /> The Fairest one you have would not make sense to the way I play it: since the device uses the number of successes rolled on the activation for attack rolls, I think it is fair to say it uses those successes for any rolls it needs to make. I also think it is fair to say that when the Contract says that by buying an object as a Retainer &quot;it stays permanently animated&quot;, that we take that to mean the Contract's duration changes to &quot;permanent&quot;. So when you get an Exceptional Success, the object's dice pool is 5, which trumps a 2-Dot Retainer's dicepool of 4 for actions they are good at. Specific beats general, so the specific rules for how the object is animated should beat the general merit rules here. That means that upgrading the Retainer merit from 1 to 2 dots essentially does nothing. If you were allowed to upgrade it to 5 there could be a benefit to it. I'd allow my players to upgrade the merit by default: it might be an item, but they could be &quot;investing&quot; more of themselves/their power into it. A 5-dot floating gun Retainer will hardly be overpowered, considering that it will be <i>much</i> more limited than a normal 5-dot Retainer. Simply by virtue of not having any hands, for one...<br /> <br /> I like your Ogre one though, it's very similar to mine but I think yours is slightly better.<ul><li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Darkling:</b> </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Darkling may whisper the command to the device along with a simple triggering event described in no more than a few words. When doing so, the duration of the Contract becomes 1 chapter, and the device lays dormant until the specified trigger occurs, then executes the command. The Contract ends once the command has been executed, or at the end of the scene during which the trigger occurred.</span></span><ul><li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Example: Used on a car with the command \u201cWhen driving next to a cliff, drive off of it.\u201d</i></span></span></li> </ul></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Elemental:</b> </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">By spending an additional willpower point, the toys are infused with the boundless energy that drives the Unbound forward. When making an attack (or when otherwise called upon to make a roll) the object uses twice the Contract\u2019s activation successes instead of the normal amount.</span></span></li> <li><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Fairest: </span></span></b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Fairest gains two free merit dots that may only be used to buy devices animated by this Contract as Retainers. They may buy any device they animate as a one-dot Retainer with these merit dots, rather than needing to roll an exceptional success to do so. If they want more than 2 devices as Retainers at a time, they must purchase them as normal.</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Ogre:</b> </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Ogre\u2019s toughness infuses any device he animates, increasing their durability by two, to a maximum of five. (If the object\u2019s durability is already equal to or higher than five, it doesn\u2019t change.)</span></span></span></span></li> </ul><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Homebrew</b><br /> - CtL 2E: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1290961-additional-seeming-benefits-for-contracts-2e?p=1454478#post1454478\" target=\"_blank\">Seeming Benefits for every Contract</a>","edited":{"userid":1292,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","user":"Unahim","date":". 01-02-2020, 10:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","name":"Unahim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1292_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1080"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-03T01:49:32-08:00","id":1358572,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Unahim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1357386-bonuses-for-dance-of-the-toys?p=1358401#post1358401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm past the halfway point of coming up with 4 Seeming bonuses for every Contract, below are the ones I have for Dance of the Toys. I like my Darkling &amp; Elemental ones, so you could check them out if you're looking to replace yours (and a note for the Elemental one you have: objects don't normally track aggravated damage separate from any other damage, so doing that may lead to extra bookkeeping, which I try to avoid in these benefits for elegance).</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree that the aspect of Aggravated being differentiated is perhaps overly complicated for what it\u2019s meant to be for. I was simply trying to make an option for Elementals that would be useful but not <i>overly</i> useful, so an enchanted, totalled car won\u2019t be back up and running by leaving it in a sand dune after a few hours.<br /> <br /> I do appreciate you sharing your own ideas here.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Fairest one you have would not make sense to the way I play it: since the device uses the number of successes rolled on the activation for attack rolls, I think it is fair to say it uses those successes for any rolls it needs to make. I also think it is fair to say that when the Contract says that by buying an object as a Retainer &quot;it stays permanently animated&quot;, that we take that to mean the Contract's duration changes to &quot;permanent&quot;. So when you get an Exceptional Success, the object's dice pool is 5, which trumps a 2-Dot Retainer's dicepool of 4 for actions they are good at. Specific beats general, so the specific rules for how the object is animated should beat the general merit rules here. That means that upgrading the Retainer merit from 1 to 2 dots essentially does nothing. If you were allowed to upgrade it to 5 there could be a benefit to it. I'd allow my players to upgrade the merit by default: it might be an item, but they could be &quot;investing&quot; more of themselves/their power into it. A 5-dot floating gun Retainer will hardly be overpowered, considering that it will be <i>much</i> more limited than a normal 5-dot Retainer. Simply by virtue of not having any hands, for one... </div> </div> </div>This is where our design sensibilities differ. While your idea of a permanently enchanted device having a die pool of 5 owing to an exceptional success being 5 successes has merit, Changelings muddy that idea a fair bit simply because they can force an exceptional success for a contract at any point by scouring their Mask. Theoretically a Changeling could have a device with a die pool of 1 while having an exceptional success simply by having 1 success on the roll, 1 dot of Mantle and Wyrd while their Mask has been dropped. This is to say nothing about what happens when you achieve an exceptional success by rolling more than 5 successes, which theoretically should result in the permanent enchantment having a larger die pool than 5 successes.<br /> <br /> Simply put, this reads to me as requiring a lot of bookkeeping unless you make it a ubiquitous die pool of 5 no matter how you got the permanent enchantment, which makes scouring the Mask the ideal method of acquisition.<br /> <br /> However, I do have an answer from Meghan that sheds some light on why I went the route I did;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Meghan Fitzgerald</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1050476-ask-a-simple-question-get-a-simple-answer-changeling-edition?p=1357098#post1357098\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Correct--it no longer requires an invocation roll (or Glamour/Loophole), and the animated device can go wherever. It functions just like any other Retainer, except it's not a person. You still retain the Seeming benefits. The main benefit to spending the Experience to buy it as a Merit is that it won't cost you any Glamour and it will stay animated even if it gets out of range; it'll just keep doing the last thing you told it to do until it can't anymore or you give it another command. And Changeling players are always coming up with creative ways to use what they've got, so I'm sure there are other ones I'm not thinking of right now. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>The point in question is about how the device functions \u201cjust like any other Retainer, except it\u2019s not a person\u201d. Since a Retainer\u2019s die pool is twice their dot rating for their field of expertise and just their dot rating for anything outside it, this makes me think that one doing commands for their Changeling falls into that range. If this results in the die pool going lower than it was initially activating the contract, it is offset by the fact that the enchantment works outside the contract\u2019s normal range. However, by default it seems odd to be able to increase the power of the enchantment when the device only does what it\u2019s told - even with the Beast\u2019s seeming benefit, it still only changes how it <i>interprets and executes</i> the orders. How would the Changeling go about increasing the magic and the competence of the device when the closest comparable magical objects, Tokens, don\u2019t (currently) have a similar means to increase their power beyond possibly Hedgespun Items?<br /> <br /> Granted, one could work by making an exceptional success on the contract on the same object over and over again to do this, but this feels like a lackadaisical method of forcibly gaining a high dot value Retainer.<br /> <br /> Please note that this isn\u2019t meant to be an argument; I really do appreciate you putting forward your own ideas to try and spur my own (which is has, as shown below). This is more my sense of logic in why only the Fairest and those taught by them have the ability to increase their enchanted object Retainer\u2019s competence when it otherwise has no mind about it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Unahim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1357386-bonuses-for-dance-of-the-toys?p=1358401#post1358401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><ul><li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Darkling:</b> </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Darkling may whisper the command to the device along with a simple triggering event described in no more than a few words. When doing so, the duration of the Contract becomes 1 chapter, and the device lays dormant until the specified trigger occurs, then executes the command. The Contract ends once the command has been executed, or at the end of the scene during which the trigger occurred.</span></span><ul><li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>Example: Used on a car with the command \u201cWhen driving next to a cliff, drive off of it.\u201d</i></span></span></li> </ul></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Elemental:</b> </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">By spending an additional willpower point, the toys are infused with the boundless energy that drives the Unbound forward. When making an attack (or when otherwise called upon to make a roll) the object uses twice the Contract\u2019s activation successes instead of the normal amount.</span></span></li> <li><b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Fairest: </span></span></b><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Fairest gains two free merit dots that may only be used to buy devices animated by this Contract as Retainers. They may buy any device they animate as a one-dot Retainer with these merit dots, rather than needing to roll an exceptional success to do so. If they want more than 2 devices as Retainers at a time, they must purchase them as normal.</span></span></li> <li><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><b>Ogre:</b> </span></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Ogre\u2019s toughness infuses any device he animates, increasing their durability by two, to a maximum of five. (If the object\u2019s durability is already equal to or higher than five, it doesn\u2019t change.)</span></span></span></span></li> </ul></div> </div> </div> </div>This is an interesting range of abilities. Darkling I fear runs exceptionally close to the Beast\u2019s benefit as it stands, what with both allowing commands to trigger on a particular circumstance. The only difference I can find is that Beast\u2019s bonus opens up to multiple/complex commands, whereas Darkling is focused on whispering and increasing the duration to a chapter rather than a scene (which can be done for an exceptional success as an alternative to taking it as a Retainer). Still, it\u2019s not a bad concept to hide a command deep within something to be triggered later.<br /> <br /> The Elemental bonus is nice and focused for combat, which Elementals seem to trend towards. Effective language for what it does and clear in how to use it. It\u2019s not what I\u2019d want for my bonus, though, since if anything it\u2019s a bit <i>too</i> effective. The die pool normally given to enchanted devices is relatively low, somewhere between 1 and 5, meaning that while they\u2019re useful as additional attackers they\u2019re not the best option. However, with this bonus it means that even 3 successes to enchant an object turns it into an above average if somewhat limited fighter, and a permanent enchantment under your own interpretation makes it a 10 dice pool attacker, normally well above what a Changeling has on their own without willpower and contracts to fund their attacks. Even if limited to a simple pistol without the Wizened bonus, all an Elemental needs to do is point it at someone and tell it to fire, and they\u2019ll get a better result than if they themselves pulled the trigger. Suffice to say, that\u2019s a little dangerous for your combat economy if you don\u2019t have an activation fee to limit how often it\u2019s used.<br /> <br /> The Fairest bonus is... not one I\u2019m fond of. Free merits are one thing if it\u2019s part of another set of merits (Mantle, Professional Training, Mystery Cult Initiate, etc.), since that leaves an avenue for an overall refund should any of the merits be lost. Making free merits part of a <i>contract</i> though? What happens if the Retainers are destroyed or the enchantments somehow purged? Does the Changeling regain those free dots for later Retainers? Are they lost forever? This is simply a matter of taste, since with a bit of extra text would fix the above confusion.<br /> <br /> Your Ogre bonus is one I have no issues with; it\u2019s clear what it does, has established limits and is nice and practical without being too much so. If I were in a game where either my bonus or yours were chosen, I wouldn\u2019t mind if one was taken over the other.<br /> <br /> <br /> I would like to reiterate that I\u2019m not trying to attack your material here. Differences in design sensibilities aside, I do appreciate you posting your own ideas here for me to go over. Its helped me focus on what I do and don\u2019t like effect wise, and helped me focus on the topic rather than get bogged down in minutia like I have before.<br /> <br /> As such, I would like to post my new Fairest bonus for consideration;<br /> <br /> <i>Fairest</i>: By spending an additional glamour when she activates the contract, the Muse imparts true will upon her enchantment. If asked for assistance, the enchanted device may count as a secondary actor for a teamwork action it could reasonably assist in, using activation successes or its Retainer bonus as the die pool for such assistance. Unless commanded otherwise, it will do this for anyone who has matched the Muse\u2019s own gentle strokes or fear-instilling strikes. The device may work as the <i>primary</i> actor for a teamwork action, with the Muse acting as a secondary actor, but this approach tends to be throwing caution to the wind even if the Fairest ties using commands, as the enchanted device rarely does things by halves.<br /> <br /> The reason I went with this being a <i>Fairest</i> bonus instead of an Elemental bonus is simple: nature is full of natural sources of energy, which flows, collects and bursts out when needed (like lightning, or volcanos, or ocean waves). The original Fairest bonus can be retooled as an Elemental bonus where the Changeling impacts some of that natural energy into the enchantment, increasing the capability of the Retainer. Meanwhile, the Fairest bonus is a natural extension of what the Fairest blessing already does, granting willpower upon someone else for their benefit...plus some added hilarity for if the player decides to leave the device in control.<br /> <br /> Thank you Unahim for your reply; it genuinely has helped me come up with an idea I\u2019m pretty satisfied with I couldn\u2019t have thought up otherwise, and given me some insight into the problems my own bonuses may have.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5803,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","user":"Taidragon","date":". 01-03-2020, 05:07 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-03T14:49:13-08:00","id":1358705,"title":"","content":"Don't worry about upsetting me by debating these! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> How else will we pool our creativity? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> I hope you likewise won't feel I'm attacking you with these thoughts of mine. ^^<br /> <br /> Your post is quite long and mine will be too; I won't quote it for brevity's sake, but I'll just respond by topic!<br /> <br /> <b>Concerning the Exceptional Success Effect</b><br /> I don't have entirely the same interpretation of Meghan's answer. This:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The main benefit to spending the Experience to buy it as a Merit is that it won't cost you any Glamour and it will stay animated even if it gets out of range </div> </div> </div>to me reads as &quot;these are the ways in which the Contract changes by making it a merit&quot;. She never mentions the dicepool changing, and I really feel that is something the Contract should have pointed out. I think tying it to the Retainer merit is more or less a way to make the effect fall under Sanctity of Merits without having to write a lot of specific rules for it, rather than meant to directly impact dicepools. It does not make sense to me, nor does it feel right to me, for a tool to suddenly <i>lose</i> dice by spending experience on it. No such downsides are mentioned in the book or by Meghan.<br /> <br /> The offsets for this decrease in dice, such as the increased range, are paid for in experience already. 1 Experience is quite a lot! That's another Seeming benefit bought, or a sizeable chunk towards a new Contract. For that price you get a Retainer who is essentially less versatile than a &quot;normal&quot; Retainer, it doesn't seem worth buying at a dicepool of 2. There are so many things an object just can't do that a human Retainer can, they're so far behind the scale in my eyes that a few extra dice are alright.<br /> <br /> You could, in theory, animate an object by stripping the mask and getting only 1 success. I tend to see stripping the mask as a big deal which paints a big target on you, so I don't see that as a very sound regular tactic. But you're not forced to buy on every exceptional success, so you can simply decide not to spend Experience on it if you strip and the roll is bad. Recasting the same Contract over and over again doesn't really fit into story structure to me (it also still does cost Glamour, of course), and the question is how much of the Exceptional Success is an effect a character should actively pursue, and how much of it should just arise naturally as a sort of &quot;extra&quot; on a roll. If a character needs a certain specific object animated badly for his character concept, I'll just let him get it at the default 5 successes during a downtime period if enough time passes. Else I see it strictly as a &quot;and the faery's magic took effect, and the stars aligned, and their bond was so great, that Benny the Pistol gained life and stayed with her forevermore&quot; deal. Changelings are whimsical, and their magic not really a science, after all. ^^<br /> <br /> Since there's no further information to determine &quot;the truth&quot;, and since it comes down to a pure judgement call in the end, we'll just have to live with our differences on this one I think. ^^<br /> <br /> <b>Concerning my Darkling Benefit</b><br /> The main benefit is indeed meant to be the increase in duration, though it's not fully an increase in duration as it's essentially &quot;asleep&quot; for a large part of that and the Contract ends early once the command is done. The trigger gives some control over it, you could see that part as a minor form of the Beast one (in that it allows you to add 1 layer of complexity to the command), but that's alright because of how well they synergise when you have <i>both</i>. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> With both combined, you can give the object a complex series of commands to follow which it will execute beyond the current scene at a predefined trigger, a trigger which can also be more complex due to the object's increased intelligence. The trigger could then even include a conditional: if A, then X, but if B, then Y. Neither really do this on their own, so I'm alright with it if parts of the Darkling seem a little similar to the Beast one, since they both play differently enough when taken on their own, but really shine when brought together.<br /> <br /> You're right about the duration on the Exceptional though, I didn't notice that when I wrote it. But that's easily fixed, the Darkling benefit merely needs an additional effect on an Exceptional Success to compensate. Perhaps it increases the duration to a full Story then, or perhaps it could read &quot;On an Exceptional Success, the Contract no longer ends at the end of a scene in which the trigger occurred; the command will continue to be executed each time the trigger occurs until the end of the Contract's duration.&quot; I like that last one, it allows you to for instance set a guard inside your home, leave, and have it be able to defend the home no matter how many scenes pass in the Chapter, and no matter how many separate intrusion attempts there are. What do you think?<br /> <br /> <b>Concerning my Elemental Bonus</b><br /> I was already debating with myself if the bonus was a bit overtuned, but left it as is to see what you'd think without me prompting it. : )<br /> I like the idea though, two possible options to change it a bit are:<br /> - Let the Elemental add Wyrd, or half Wyrd, to the dicepool. But I don't like this as much since it is still potentially a high pool at max wyrd, but feels trivial at low wyrd.<br /> - Perhaps make it &quot;The Unbound can spend a willpower point to give the device a +3 on any rolls it makes for the rest of the scene.&quot; 3 dice is manageable, it comes down to 1 success most of the time, and in this way it has a recurring cost and does not simply buff Retainer objects permanently. I think I like this option, it's often better than spending 1 willpower point on one of your own rolls, but not always, and as it is a purchased ability (Contract, + Seeming bonus itself is worth 1 experience in theory) with some requirements (the presence of an object that could damage an enemy, which is actually a <i>really </i>limited set of items without additionally purchasing the Wizened ability) that seems like a good balance. Really, if someone buys this Contract, and gets both the Elemental and Wizened boons, and spends glamour on it, and spends willpower on it, and deals with the hassle of an obviously magical effect potentially drawing the attention of Huntsmen when seen by normal humans (rumours spread, after all), then I'm fine with a floating gun with ~8 dice for a scene. : )<br /> At most I'd drop it to only a +2 perhaps, still mulling that over, though I think it's fine at +3. Opinions?<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Concerning my Fairest Bonus</b><br /> I think it is easy to adjudicate while in a game, but simply add this text to make the &quot;official&quot; stance more clear: &quot;So long as the Fairest retains both this Contract and this Seeming bonus, these merit dots are subject to Sanctity of Merits, but remain subject to the aforementioned restrictions (e.g: may only be used to turn newly animated objects into Retainers through the use of this Contract). Should the Fairest ever lose access to this Contract or this Seeming bonus, these merit dots fade, and the magic animating those objects tied to these dots immediately ends.&quot;<br /> <br /> For me it seemed clear enough, they are merits and so are covered by Sanctity of Merits (which doesn't, by RAW, have a specific exception listed in the rules for merits acquired in different ways... I don't apply it when players lose merits I gave them which they did not buy with Experience, but technically those are house rules), and they have specific restrictions on what can be bought with them, without any mention that the restrictions would somehow lift just because the dots become freed up later. But this small bit of extra text should clear it up no matter what, so you're right there, still a good addendum. ^^<br /> <br /> <b>A small note on the interplay of boons</b><br /> I try to make sure the Seeming bonuses work together in some way, so players might be excited if allies offer to teach them bonuses they don't have to Contracts they own. Not all bonuses need to fit together, some can just be alternatives or extra applications, but it is always nice if they directly click together.<br /> I think that turned out pretty well on this Contract! : )<br /> <br /> I've already spoken a bit about the interplay of the Beast&amp;Darkling ones in my version, and touched upon how great it is to add Wizened to that as well. Adding Elemental on top of that one can be great (I'd likely add a note saying that if you have both the Elemental and Darkling power, you can spend a Willpower point in advance to give the device the Elemental benefit the first time the trigger occurs, I often add notes about how I envision different bonuses to work together, the more of them you add to a Contract the higher the chance of some odd interactions and questions coming up). Adding Ogre (your version, in this case, since I like it better!) to make it even harder to stop the object is great too! All 5 of these work together. That's fun. ^^ You also don't need all of them, Darkling + Wizened is cool, as is Elemental + Beast, or Ogre + Wizened, etc... All works! : D<br /> <br /> The Fairest one then doesn't really add directly to the combo, but you get more value out of the merit dots the more of the other benefits you have, so that's good too. : )<br /> <br /> <b>Concerning Your Fairest Bonus</b><br /> Really don't want to rain on your parade when you seem so pleased with this one. D: But I don't really get it. ^^<br /> Why couldn't the device do this in its entirety with the Beast merit? I'm assuming you'd, for instance, use this on a car to help you drive it better, but if you give it an animal intelligence (which should enable it to have a pretty good, intuitive grasp of how its movement works) and command it to make helpful adjustments to your driving, then I don't see why that wouldn't work. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> I'm not against parts of a bonus resembling one another (see: Darkling benefit above xD) but in this case I can't think of a use that the Beast one couldn't replicate exactly. Or am I misunderstanding the bonus? D:","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Homebrew</b><br /> - CtL 2E: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1290961-additional-seeming-benefits-for-contracts-2e?p=1454478#post1454478\" target=\"_blank\">Seeming Benefits for every Contract</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","name":"Unahim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1292_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1080"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-05T01:20:08-08:00","id":1359034,"title":"","content":"<b>Concerning the Exceptional Success Effect</b><br /> <br /> The big charge for my interpretation is the context of the answer, for which we must go to the question I posited, that being below:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Taidragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1050476-ask-a-simple-question-get-a-simple-answer-changeling-edition?p=1356267#post1356267\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am having some difficulty appreciating the change that occurs in an object when it becomes permanently animated as a one dot Retainer via Dance Of The Toys. Does it no longer require an activation roll for accepting commands? Will it follow commands (or a single command) if it leaves the normal control area? Would it still benefit from any Seeming bonuses such as the ability to float at walking speed if a Wizened animated it?<br /> <br /> Basically I want to appreciate the benefit of spending an experience on something as opposed to evoking the contract over and over again to almost certainly get a better benefit than a Retainer\u2019s 2 dice to fulfill a task.</div> </div> </div> </div>For a question regarding what the benefit is for taking a one dot Retainer is <i>instead</i> of evoking the contract to almost inevitably get a larger bonus, Meghan didn't seem to be particularly forthright in correcting my misapprehension about having to <i>choose</i> between a large Success based die pool or the Retainer's benefit of &quot;has a die pool twice the Retainer's dot rating for something within their wheelhouse&quot;. While it's not impossible that she simply missed that aspect of the question and thus wasn't answering it, it's not as though I buried it what with it being at the very bottom of the question quote in its own sentence.<br /> <br /> Still, I agree that this is probably something best left to a difference of opinion lest the thread burns from an argument over a particular facet of a single power.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>On your Darkling Benefit</b><br /> <br /> Sad to say, but I still don't think it's separate enough from an exceptionally succeeding Beast benefit for my liking. While I agree that complementary benefits are very nice (Tatterdemalion's Workshop and Shared Burden are fine with one benefit or the other, but are king with both) your Darkling benefit feels like it's simply allowing for the exceptional success to be done easier with less benefits for doing so.<br /> <br /> I will say that it's fine; it's functional, clearly written out and unlikely to break the bank like mine potentially could (without the activation cost) for a Darkling in a dim space with a flashlight. However, the capacity for what a Darkling could do is quite potent with enchanting a device, such as perhaps working on objects without moving parts but a definite function, like a smartphone, or allowing the Changeling to possess the object instead to use it as a temporary body. It's why I opted to go for range for a touch based power, much like for a Wizened with Touch Of Wrath. However, I won't say your idea for this power is bad, just a bit limited.<br /> <br /> <b>On your Elemental Bonus</b><br /> <br /> As I noted before, your benefit works alright if it has an activation cost to prevent the enchantment from making a player obsolete in using the device themselves. Limiting the benefit to simply a +2 or +3 to the device's die rolls works well since it means even a Fae Touched could make use of the benefit. Even without the ability to permanently increase a permanently enchanted device's Retainer rating and its die pool normally being twice the Retainer's rating, this gives a nice boost to the device's capabilities. Works well, just not enough to make me replace my own with it.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Concerning your Fairest Bonus</b><br /> <br /> You make a fair point there (no pun intended), but I'm still not fond of it simply because it gives free merits as opposed to... well, something that plays around with the contract itself. It gives permanence without cost; whenever the other contracts and their bonuses give access to merits, it's inevitably temporary or requires an experience to keep it around (and often has a requirement to use them even then). As with the Darkling benefit above, it's functional and aside from that one niggle it's written out well enough not to break the game by having it in one, but not what I feel really encapsulates what a Fairest can do with this kind of power. Difference of opinion reigns here.<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Concerning <i>my</i> Fairest Bonus</b><br /> <br /> The main benefit of the bonus is that it allows the object to not just help the user but benefit others who know how to ask the device to help them during the scene, that being kind/terrible and asking for their assistance. It goes beyond the Beast benefit too, since &quot;Help/work anyone with a white shirt&quot; is likely outside of the capabilities of an animal style intelligence when &quot;help&quot; is highly context sensitive. An enchanted car can &quot;help&quot; someone numerous ways, be it by getting them in out of the cold and warmed up, driving them down to the nearest hospital or simply playing a pleasant tune via the radio while they cry their heart out from a particularly bad revelation, but it seems unlikely that &quot;animal-like intelligence, allowing [the original contract user] to give it multiple <i>or</i> more complex commands to follow&quot; would cover the ability to allow someone else to get the car to help them outside of a very particular instance of use.<br /> <br /> The benefit is meant to convey that if the device could help with something and it's not being commanded by the contract's power itself, it is actively trying to do so. This can range from a wheelchair moving its wheels to assist the user around in a library while the person sitting in it maneuvers and holds up the books for their research attempt, a gun helping correct for any inaccuracies while sniping genocidal Hedge ferrets from a long distance off, or a pair of scissors ensuring that the five hundred and two stars representing the entire Freehold are all cut exactly as the Autumn Queen specified and not cutting over the lines; these are all things that could be done with a direct command with the Beast benefit, sure, but this grants the ability to do more without it being told to. Combined with the Beast benefit, it gives the device a sense of real life and mind, able to act for others beyond just doing as told specific actions by the original enchanter, like how a Fairest grants others a boost of Willpower without the other needing to spend their own.<br /> <br /> Granted, now that I am out of <a href=\"https://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/TheMadnessPlace\" target=\"_blank\">the madness place</a>, I can see this being a bit fiddly with making actions with lots of dice rolls getting even <i>more</i> dice rolls, which, as learned by playing in a game with another using Download Knowledge for <i>every</i> plausible benefit on most dice rolls, can <i>really slow a scene down</i>. This goes in particular if someone animates a device (requiring a die roll for activation), asks it for help in an action (which is another die roll to get the bonus) and then more die rolls for the action requiring this help. I might make this only for permanent/chapter long enchantments to avoid the initial roll, in that case, though that does make the initial Retainer bonus without any effects to buy more Retainer dots rather minimal... maybe I'll just make it a +2 or +3 when it's a secondary actor and its full bonus rolled when it's the primary actor.<br /> <br /> Though not explicitly written (which I should have noted), the Fairest bonus is also meant to work with the equipment bonus of the device itself, but that might also be a bit much when it's already acting alongside you... I may simply be too obsessed with recreating a particular famous racing car for my own good.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-09T03:27:40-08:00","id":1359870,"title":"","content":"Tad embarrassed, both about the double post and not thinking this idea sooner, but I just thought of a more practical bonus for either Fairest or (more fitting) Elemental bonus: the enchanted device shall perform its mundane uses in the Hedge as it would in the mundane realm.<br /> <br /> The object is now enchanted, whether briefly or permanently, so it will be immunized to the rigours of the Hedge that cause mundane objects to act out. A flashlight will project light normally, a cellphone may lack service or have a signal depending on the hedge gates currently open in the area, and a sword... well it\u2019s not really a \u201cdevice\u201d so it is probably out of this contract\u2019s range. Still, something niche but handy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-16T04:37:44-08:00","id":1361252,"title":"","content":"Been bit busy recently, sorry for not having been able to chime in sooner. ^^<br /> It sounds like we're both mostly at a &quot;your ideas are good but maybe not for me&quot; point, so I won't retread that ground. ^^<br /> <br /> This latest bonus you suggest seems workable, but I don't remember it being stated that flashlights and the like &quot;act out&quot; in the Hedge? I'm away from books at the moment, so I can't check. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/redface.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Embarrassment\" smilieid=\"2\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Homebrew</b><br /> - CtL 2E: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1290961-additional-seeming-benefits-for-contracts-2e?p=1454478#post1454478\" target=\"_blank\">Seeming Benefits for every Contract</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","name":"Unahim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1292_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1080"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-16T07:40:29-08:00","id":1361274,"title":"","content":"This portion on page 69;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Objects you carry into the Hedge may continue to work, but will become temperamental and whimsical. A flashlight may throw light, but as a lantern or a candle or a cold flame cradled in your palm. A phone might make contact, but to the person you last told a secret or with your voice translated into a forgotten tongue. An object may choose to obey the letter of the law rather than the spirit, or interpret your actions as metaphoric desires. A lit path may glow with a sudden beam of sunshine, or become alight with flame. A sword might become a serpent in your hand, poised to strike the warrior as well as the adversary. </div> </div> </div>Admittedly I don\u2019t remember this having much presence in the book/gameline at the moment, but it\u2019s still somewhat annoying to deal with.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-01-29T04:57:04-08:00","id":1363945,"title":"","content":"Ah, cool. ^^ I missed that! A benefit that help you deal with this is pretty good then.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Homebrew</b><br /> - CtL 2E: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1290961-additional-seeming-benefits-for-contracts-2e?p=1454478#post1454478\" target=\"_blank\">Seeming Benefits for every Contract</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","name":"Unahim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1292_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1080"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}